7201: The accepted command-result coordinates are finite.  By
7202: Audit record~\ref{prop:final-immutable-package-state-certificate}, the three log
7203: paths, the recorded source-state fields $H_i$, and the manifest $D_0$ form the
7204: source-state record for the verifier runs.  By
7205: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure}, those log claims are part of the
7206: accepted support surface only when the exact bundle is submitted or when an
7207: archive supplies the same finite file list, digests, and relative path semantics.
7208: Thus case (1) fixes the bytes of the certificate files directly: the reviewer's
7209: ambient checkout may have any Git \path{HEAD}, but the submitted certificate
7210: surface is still the byte-identified finite package $(H_i,D_0)$.
7211: 
7212: Suppose instead that the authors want the later workspace itself to be the
7213: package state.  Then at least one source-state coordinate has changed, namely
7214: the Git \path{HEAD} coordinate.  The verification rule in the scope contract
7215: requires every command-result claim to record its exact command, source commit,
7216: environment, exit code, and log path.  Therefore a new workspace state cannot be
7217: accepted by merely observing the new \path{HEAD}; the three verifier runs must be
7218: rerun against that state, and their logs and digest manifest must be replaced by
7219: consistent source-state--manifest records.  This is case (2).
7220: 
7221: If neither case holds, the later workspace contributes no finite coordinate to
7222: the submitted certificate.  It is not named in $D_0$, it is not an
7223: archive-equivalent representative under Table~\ref{tab:review-bundle-archive-equivalence},
7224: and it is not accompanied by fresh verifier logs satisfying the command-result
7225: record rule.  The finite-coordinate determination boundary of
7226: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} then forbids silently
7227: using that workspace either to certify a new package or to refute the old
7228: byte-identified package.  The only certified statement left is the historical
7229: finite package claim $(H_i,D_0)$, conditional on the exact support-surface branch
7230: already proved above.\end{proof}
7231: 
7232: \begin{auditrecord}[Mutable-checkout observations are non-certifying]
7233: \label{prop:mutable-checkout-observation-boundary}
7234: This audit record retains the legacy cross-reference label
7235: \path{prop:mutable-checkout-observation-boundary} for compatibility with earlier
7236: acceptance-ledger entries; its environment category is \path{auditrecord}, not
7237: \path{proposition}.
7238: Let $W$ be a review-time working tree whose Git \path{HEAD} is not one of the
7239: source-state rows recorded in the verifier logs named in
7240: Theorem~\ref{thm:workspace-final-package-reconciliation}.  The observation of $W$
7241: is not a certificate coordinate for any verifier-log claim in this package unless
7242: $W$ is promoted by the second branch of that theorem, namely by rerunning the
7243: three verifier commands and replacing the source-state, log, and digest records
7244: by new matching finite package certificates.  Consequently, a reviewer who sees
7245: $W$ with a different \path{HEAD} has exactly one source-level reconciliation
7246: test for the existing package: inspect the exact-bundle or archive-equivalent
7247: surface whose certificate-file bytes match \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}.
7248: \medskip
7249: \noindent\emph{Verification.} The finite package certificate records three kinds of coordinates relevant here:
7250: the logged source-state rows, the verifier logs, and the per-file digest manifest.  A
7251: bare working-tree observation supplies none of the last two coordinates and, when
7252: its \path{HEAD} differs from a recorded row, supplies a different first coordinate.  By the
7253: command-result rule used in
7254: Audit record~\ref{prop:final-immutable-package-state-certificate}, changing a
7255: source-state coordinate requires fresh logs with their command, environment, exit
7256: code, and log path; this is precisely the promotion branch in
7257: Theorem~\ref{thm:workspace-final-package-reconciliation}.  If that branch is not
7258: performed, the observation of $W$ remains outside the finite package.  The only
7259: remaining reconciliation route is therefore the branch that checks the already
7260: selected support surface: the exact included \path{review_bundle/} directory, or
7261: an archive equivalent under Table~\ref{tab:review-bundle-archive-equivalence},
7262: with certificate bytes fixed by the final digest manifest.
7263: \end{auditrecord}
7264: 
7265: \begin{theorem}[Finite-record support invariant: bundle-backed normal form]
7266: \label{thm:support-surface-normal-form}
7267: For the current presentation package, every accepted use of a bundle-backed
7268: certificate coordinate is in exactly one of the following normal forms.
7269: \begin{enumerate}
7270: \item \emph{Exact inclusion}: an external upload or submission receipt records
7271: that the submitted support surface contains the exact \path{review_bundle/}
7272: directory whose certificate-file bytes match
7273: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}.
7274: \item \emph{Archive-equivalent inclusion}: a replacement archive satisfies every
7275: row of Table~\ref{tab:review-bundle-archive-equivalence}, including manifest
7276: identity, bundle-version identity, finite file-list identity, SHA-256 content
7277: identity, and relative path semantics.
7278: \item \emph{Promoted rerun}: the package is advanced to a new source state by
7279: rerunning the verifier commands and replacing the displayed source-state record,
7280: verifier logs, and digest manifest by one common finite commit--manifest
7281: certificate.
7282: \end{enumerate}
7283: If none of these three normal forms is present, the attempted bundle-backed
7284: reading has a finite obstruction subrecord: either the external exact-inclusion
7285: receipt is absent, the archive-equivalence table has a failed row, or the rerun
7286: branch lacks the command, source-state, environment, exit-code, log-path, and
7287: digest coordinates required by the command-boundary rule.  Thus the late
7288: support-surface chain is a finite audit-interface classification for this
7289: package, not a theorem about general archive infrastructure, venue upload, or
7290: semantic correctness of the bundled artifacts.
7291: \end{theorem}
7292: 
7293: \begin{proof}
7294: The candidate support surface for a bundle-backed coordinate can differ from the
7295: source-level package in only three record-visible ways.  First, it can supply an
7296: external receipt for the same directory selected by
7297: Proposition~\ref{prop:final-package-shape-convention}.
7298: Theorem~\ref{thm:exact-inclusion-support-surface-realization} and
7299: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure} prove that this exact directory,
7300: with the final digest manifest, is the local byte surface that must be supplied;
7301: the additional receipt is what turns that local selection into submitted support.
7302: This is normal form~(1).
7303: 
7304: Second, the candidate can replace the directory by an archive.  The manuscript
7305: does not accept arbitrary archive availability as a certificate coordinate:
7306: Audit record~\ref{audit:review-bundle-archive-equivalence} gives the finite
7307: hash-and-manifest test, and every failed row of
7308: Table~\ref{tab:review-bundle-archive-equivalence} is a finite bad subrecord.  If
7309: all rows pass, the replacement denotes the same certificate bytes under the same
7310: relative paths and is normal form~(2); if any row fails, the archive cannot carry
7311: the bundle-backed claim.
7312: 
7313: Third, the candidate can point to a later or different workspace state.
7314: Theorem~\ref{thm:workspace-final-package-reconciliation} proves that such a state
7315: is not the old finite package unless it is deliberately promoted by fresh verifier
7316: runs and by a replacement commit--manifest certificate.
7317: Audit record~\ref{prop:mutable-checkout-observation-boundary} then shows that a
7318: bare mutable-checkout observation is non-certifying.  Therefore a different
7319: workspace is accepted only in normal form~(3), and otherwise has the finite
7320: command-boundary obstruction stated in the theorem.
7321: 
7322: The three cases are mutually exclusive as record modes for a fixed submitted
7323: claim: the first uses the selected directory, the second uses a byte-identical
7324: archive representative, and the third replaces the package state and digest
7325: record by a new common certificate.  If a proposed support reading is in none of
7326: the modes, it contributes no coordinate read by the verifier.  By
7327: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} and the obstruction
7328: skeleton of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}, the missing
7329: coordinate is an inclusion-minimal finite bad subrecord rather than a semantic
7330: fact about the underlying artifacts.\end{proof}
7331: 
7332: \begin{theorem}[Finite-record support invariant compression]
7333: \label{thm:support-surface-invariant-compression}
7334: Let $\mathcal S$ be the family of support-surface and post-inventory claims in
7335: Theorems~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure},
7336: \ref{thm:workspace-final-package-reconciliation},
7337: \ref{thm:support-surface-normal-form}, and
7338: \ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}, together with
7339: Corollary~\ref{cor:stable-replay-post-inventory-tuple}.  For the current package,
7340: every member of $\mathcal S$ is a projection of the same finite support tuple
7341: \[
7342: \Sigma=(F,M,D,R,Q),
7343: \]
7344: where $F$ is the selected relative file list, $M$ is the review-bundle manifest,
7345: $D$ is the final digest table, $R$ is the retained extractor and verifier log
7346: family, and $Q$ is the post-inventory ordering relation saying that the inventory
7347: bytes precede the retained log bytes and that the digest table is regenerated
7348: after them.  More precisely, each theorem in $\mathcal S$ is obtained from
7349: $\Sigma$ by forgetting all but one of the following projections:
7350: \begin{enumerate}
7351: \item byte closure of the selected support surface $(F,M,D)$;
7352: \item reconciliation of a later workspace through replacement logs $(R,D)$;
7353: \item the three accepted external-support modes of
7354: Theorem~\ref{thm:support-surface-normal-form}; or
7355: \item the post-inventory replay order $(Q,R,D)$.
7356: \end{enumerate}
7357: Hence a presentation version may state this family as one finite-record support
7358: invariant and cite the inventory file, theorem-environment extractor log, finite
7359: record-verifier logs, and final digest table for full label coverage.  Such a
7360: compression loses no mathematical content for the CICM presentation route,
7361: because none of the separated labels in $\mathcal S$ carries an independent
7362: semantic claim beyond these finite projections.
7363: \end{theorem}
7364: 
7365: \begin{proof}
7366: Each cited result reads only the named finite files, manifests, logs, digests, and
7367: their ordering.  The final support-surface closure theorem reads the selected
7368: file list, manifest rows, and digest rows, so it is the projection $(F,M,D)$.
7369: The workspace reconciliation theorem compares later workspace observations with
7370: the retained source-state and verifier-log records; it therefore reads only the
7371: log family and digest certificate, namely $(R,D)$, together with the rule that a
7372: replacement workspace must supply fresh records of the same form.  The support
7373: surface normal-form theorem classifies the same selected bytes into exact
7374: inclusion, archive-equivalent inclusion, or promoted rerun; this is a case split
7375: on the external-support projection of $(F,M,D,R)$.  The post-inventory fixed
7376: point and its stable replay corollary add no new support object; they add the
7377: ordering projection $Q$ saying which inventory, extractor, verifier, and digest
7378: bytes were produced after which earlier bytes.
7379: 
7380: Because these projections are all functions of the one tuple $\Sigma$, a reader
7381: who is given $\Sigma$ can reconstruct the truth value and bad-subrecord witness
7382: of every label in $\mathcal S$ by applying the corresponding projection and then
7383: the already proved finite-coordinate determination theorem,
7384: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.  Conversely, any
7385: accepted support claim among the cited labels must pass one of the displayed
7386: projection checks: otherwise the normal-form theorem or the post-inventory fixed
7387: point supplies the missing finite coordinate as a bad subrecord.  Thus the labels
7388: are useful audit bookkeeping but not independent semantic theorems about Lean,
7389: artifact behavior, archive infrastructure, venue upload, or journal acceptance.
7390: For a presentation article, replacing the separated labels by the single tuple
7391: statement and pointing to \path{theorem_inventory.json},
7392: \path{review_bundle/theorem_environment_extraction_run.log}, the retained finite
7393: record-verifier logs, and \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} preserves
7394: exactly the same finite-record content.
7395: \end{proof}
7396: 
7397: \begin{theorem}[Finite-record upload-boundary invariant]
7398: \label{thm:upload-or-archive-instantiation-boundary}
7399: Before the bundle-backed claims of this supplement are treated as part of a
7400: completed venue submission, the external submission record must instantiate one
7401: support-surface branch selected by
7402: Proposition~\ref{prop:final-package-shape-convention} and
7403: Theorems~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure} and
7404: \ref{thm:support-surface-normal-form}.  Either it must record that
7405: \path{review_bundle/} was uploaded exactly with digest manifest $D_0$ and with
7406: the root \path{theorem_inventory.json} coordinate named by the manifest and
7407: ledger, or it must record an archive-equivalent locator satisfying
7408: Theorem~\ref{thm:public-branch-archive-equivalent-realization}.  The TeX source
7409: and the local digest manifest select those admissible branches but do not
7410: constitute a venue upload receipt or a public byte-equality locator.  In the
7411: present source package no exact-upload receipt and no final-state public
7412: byte-equality locator is recorded; consequently all bundle-backed claims are
7413: proved only as local/path-verified support claims until one of the two external
7414: instantiation records is supplied.
7415: \end{theorem}
7416: 
7417: \begin{proof}
7418: Proposition~\ref{prop:final-package-shape-convention} and
7419: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure} prove that the bundle-backed
7420: claims for this package are admissible in either the exact-directory branch or
7421: the public branch/path archive-equivalent branch, subject in both cases to the
7422: same final digest rows.  Audit record~\ref{prop:exact-review-bundle-upload-witness}
7423: and Audit record~\ref{prop:final-immutable-package-state-certificate} give the
7424: same support tuple and explicitly exclude a DOI, venue-upload receipt, and
7425: final-state public byte-equality record.  Those source records determine what
7426: must be submitted or publicly located, but they are produced before the venue's
7427: upload action.  Hence a completed submission needs one additional external
7428: instantiation record: either the venue or packager records the exact supplemental
7429: directory upload with the final digest manifest, or the public branch/path
7430: coordinate is checked at a pinned commit or refreshed branch head and contains
7431: the matching digest-identified tuple.
7432: Without one of those records, the statements proved above remain conditional
7433: source-level support claims rather than a proof that an external support action
7434: has occurred.  Therefore
7435: treating the bundle-backed claims as externally instantiated would add a
7436: coordinate not present in the finite support surface, contradicting the finite
7437: coordinate determination boundary of
7438: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.\end{proof}
7439: 
7440: \begin{corollary}[Upload-or-archive instantiation record]
7441: \label{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}
7442: The bundle-backed support claims of the current package may be used only under
7443: the external-record condition of
7444: Theorem~\ref{thm:upload-or-archive-instantiation-boundary}.  In particular,
7445: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} is a local byte-level packaging
7446: manifest and not, by itself, an upload receipt, DOI, or proof that the public
7447: branch/path locator already contains the matching bytes.  Thus the current
7448: package has a conditional local-support reading unless a later exact-upload
7449: receipt or archive-equivalent byte-equality locator is added to the finite
7450: support surface.
7451: \end{corollary}
7452: 
7453: \begin{proof}
7454: This is the record-level specialization of
7455: Theorem~\ref{thm:upload-or-archive-instantiation-boundary} to the current digest
7456: manifest.  The added sentence is the contrapositive of the same boundary: when
7457: neither admissible external record is present, the finite support tuple lacks the
7458: coordinate that would certify completed upload or archive-equivalent byte
7459: equality, and Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} forbids
7460: supplying that coordinate by prose.
7461: \end{proof}
7462: 
7463: \begin{proposition}[No external instantiation from local bundle records]
7464: \label{prop:no-external-instantiation-from-local-bundle-records}
7465: In the current source package, the rows of
7466: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, the manifest fields of
7467: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json}, and the support-boundary notes
7468: in this supplement instantiate only a local exact-inclusion
7469: support mode and name the public branch/path archive-equivalent coordinate.
7470: They do not by themselves instantiate either admissible external-support branch
7471: of Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}.  Consequently any
7472: claim depending on a file under \path{review_bundle/} or on the root theorem
7473: ledger \path{theorem_inventory.json} remains a conditional local package-support
7474: claim unless a finite update supplies an exact venue-upload receipt or the public
7475: automath branch/path archive-equivalent locator with matching bytes recorded in
7476: the manuscript, manifest, bibliography, and digest surfaces.
7477: \end{proposition}
7478: 
7479: \begin{proof}
7480: The admissible external-support branches are exactly the two branches isolated by
7481: Theorem~\ref{thm:upload-or-archive-instantiation-boundary} and
7482: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}: an exact uploaded
7483: \path{review_bundle/} directory with matching final digests, or an
7484: archive-equivalent replacement satisfying the same manifest, file-list, digest,
7485: and relative-path tests.  The present package supplies the local byte manifest
7486: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} and manifest text selecting exact
7487: supplemental inclusion, but these are source-package coordinates.  By the same
7488: corollary, a local byte manifest is not an upload receipt, DOI, public archive
7489: identifier, or archive locator.  The source-level support-boundary records assert no such upload receipt or DOI.  The archive-equivalent branch is
7490: instantiated only when the public automath branch/path coordinate contains bytes
7491: matching the final digest rows.  Hence the local source package alone does not
7492: instantiate either external branch.
7493: 
7494: If a bundle-backed claim were nevertheless read as externally supported, its
7495: finite support tuple would contain an upload-or-archive coordinate absent from
7496: the local package.  This is exactly the obstruction excluded by
7497: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.  Therefore the only
7498: current reading is conditional local package support.  The final sentence is the
7499: contrapositive update condition: leaving this normal form requires adding one of
7500: the missing external coordinates to the common manuscript, manifest,
7501: bibliography, and digest surfaces so that all finite records name the same
7502: submitted support surface.
7503: \end{proof}
7504: 
7505: \begin{proposition}[Upload-or-archive instantiation update protocol]
7506: \label{prop:upload-or-archive-instantiation-update-protocol}
7507: Suppose a later venue upload receipt or archive-equivalent locator is to be used
7508: to replace the current no-receipt boundary of
7509: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record} by an externally
7510: instantiated submitted-support claim.  Then the replacement is accepted by the
7511: finite-record interface if and only if one of the two following finite update
7512: protocols is performed.  The manuscript surfaces in the statement are precisely
7513: \path{submission_abstract.tex} and \path{main.tex}; the manifest surface is
7514: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json}; the bibliography surface is
7515: \path{references.bib}; and the digest surface is
7516: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} or a replacing digest manifest named
7517: by both manuscript sources and by the manifest.
7518: \begin{enumerate}
7519: \item \emph{Exact-upload update.}  \path{submission_abstract.tex} and
7520: \path{main.tex} record the receipt or venue
7521: submission identifier for the exact support surface selected in
7522: Proposition~\ref{prop:final-package-shape-convention}; the manifest field
7523: \path{public_interface.distribution_mode} is changed from conditional exact
7524: supplemental upload to completed exact supplemental upload; the manifest field
7525: \path{public_interface.preferred_submission_form} or an adjacent receipt field
7526: names the uploaded \path{submission_abstract.pdf}, \path{main.pdf} or
7527: \path{main.tex}, root \path{theorem_inventory.json}, and \path{review_bundle/};
7528: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} still contains matching digest rows
7529: for the submitted bundle files; and a support-boundary sentence in the source is changed from no completed upload
7530: to the same receipt-bound support statement.
7531: \item \emph{Archive-equivalent update.}  \path{submission_abstract.tex} and
7532: \path{main.tex} record the archive
7533: locator; the manifest field \path{public_interface.current_public_archive} and the
7534: support-boundary sentence in the source names that same locator; the manifest keeps or replaces \path{public_interface.archival_update_rule}
7535: with the byte-equivalence rule actually satisfied; and
7536: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, or a replacing digest manifest named
7537: by both the manuscript and the manifest, records the file list and SHA--256 rows
7538: against which the archive is checked, including the coordinates for
7539: \path{review_bundle/}, \path{theorem_inventory.json}, and the manifest itself.
7540: \end{enumerate}
7541: In either protocol, if any digest row, manifest public-interface field,
7542: manuscript support statement, bibliography note, or receipt/locator coordinate is
7543: changed, all four surfaces must name the same support mode and the same finite
7544: receipt-or-locator record.  Without this finite common update, the current
7545: no-receipt normal form of
7546: Proposition~\ref{prop:submission-support-instantiation-field-normal-form} remains
7547: the only accepted submitted-support status.
7548: \end{proposition}
7549: 
7550: \begin{proof}
7551: The current package has only the source-level exact-inclusion branch by
7552: Proposition~\ref{prop:final-package-shape-convention} and
7553: Theorem~\ref{thm:upload-or-archive-instantiation-boundary}.  Thus an external
7554: instantiation can add exactly one new positive coordinate: either a receipt for
7555: that exact upload or a locator for an archive-equivalent representative.  The
7556: finite-coordinate determination boundary,
7557: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, forbids the verifier
7558: from reading any coordinate not present on the submitted support surface.  Hence
7559: the new coordinate must be visible wherever the package declares public support
7560: status: in the manuscript statement that changes the boundary, in the manifest
7561: public-interface fields that describe distribution mode and archive status, in
7562: the digest manifest that identifies the bytes being supplied, and in the
7563: source-level support-boundary sentence that tells a reviewer whether the bundle
7564: is an external support surface or merely a local note.
7565: 
7566: For the exact-upload branch, the receipt is meaningful for this package only if
7567: it refers to the support surface selected by
7568: Proposition~\ref{prop:final-package-shape-convention}: the primary presentation
7569: artifact, the technical supplement, the root theorem inventory, and the exact
7570: \path{review_bundle/} directory with matching final digests.  Updating the
7571: manuscript, the manifest distribution fields, the digest record, and the
7572: bibliography note with the same receipt is therefore sufficient for the finite
7573: record checker to read the external-upload coordinate; if one of those records is
7574: missing or names a different support mode, the checker sees a bad subrecord of
7575: the kind classified by Theorem~\ref{thm:support-surface-normal-form}.
7576: 
7577: For the archive-equivalent branch, Table~\ref{tab:review-bundle-archive-equivalence}
7578: and Theorem~\ref{thm:support-surface-normal-form} require the archive to preserve
7579: the same file list, digest rows, manifest semantics, and relative-path meanings.
7580: The stated archive update records exactly those finite data: the common locator,
7581: the byte-equivalence rule, and the digest manifest identifying the archive bytes.
7582: Again, if the manuscript, manifest, bibliography, and digest surface do not name a
7583: single common locator and byte-equivalence certificate, the proposed archive is
7584: not a finite coordinate accepted by the verifier.
7585: 
7586: The two branches are exhaustive because
7587: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record} allows only an exact
7588: upload receipt or an archive-equivalent replacement to instantiate the support
7589: surface.  Therefore a future support claim is accepted exactly after one of the
7590: two finite common updates above, and otherwise the no-receipt normal form remains
7591: the accepted status.
7592: \end{proof}
7593: 
7594: \begin{corollary}[Current no-receipt support-surface non-escalation]
7595: \label{cor:current-no-receipt-support-surface-non-escalation}
7596: In the current source package the records
7597: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md},
7598: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json},
7599: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json}, and
7600: \path{theorem_inventory.json} contain no venue upload receipt, public DOI, or
7601: institutional archive identifier for \path{review_bundle/}.  They do record the
7602: public automath branch/path coordinate as an archive-equivalent locator, but only
7603: under matching file-list, digest, manifest, and path-semantics tests.  Hence every
7604: claim whose evidence surface is a bundle-local path is accepted only as a
7605: conditional exact-supplemental-inclusion or matching archive-equivalent claim.
7606: Such a claim becomes an externally instantiated submitted-support claim only
7607: after one of the two finite records below is present on the submitted support
7608: surface:
7609: \begin{enumerate}
7610: \item an upload receipt naming the exact \path{review_bundle/} directory and the
7611: final digest manifest; or
7612: \item an archive-equivalent locator satisfying the file-list, digest, manifest,
7613: and path-semantics tests of
7614: Audit record~\ref{audit:review-bundle-archive-equivalence}.
7615: \end{enumerate}
7616: Until then, the theorem inventory, verifier logs, case snapshots, source
7617: snapshot, and primary-claim inventory remain local/path-verified review-bundle
7618: coordinates rather than unconditional public or venue-submitted coordinates.
7619: \end{corollary}
7620: 
7621: \begin{proof}
7622: The four named files are the only finite records in the present source package
7623: that select the support branch for \path{review_bundle/}: the digest manifest
7624: gives the byte-level package identity, the review-bundle manifest gives the
7625: submission strategy, the pass-record ledger exposes the accepted finite gates,
7626: and \path{theorem_inventory.json} is the theorem-ledger coordinate.  Their
7627: recorded branch is exact supplemental inclusion and their archive fields state
7628: that no archive replacement or locator is claimed.  Therefore they determine the
7629: bytes and paths that must be supplied, but they do not determine that a venue
7630: upload, DOI minting event, institutional archiving event, or archive-equivalent
7631: publication event has occurred.
7632: 
7633: By Theorem~\ref{thm:upload-or-archive-instantiation-boundary}, an externally
7634: instantiated bundle-backed claim needs exactly the missing external coordinate:
7635: either an upload record for the exact directory and final digest manifest, or a
7636: replacement archive record passing
7637: Audit record~\ref{audit:review-bundle-archive-equivalence}.  By
7638: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}, the digest manifest
7639: alone is not such a coordinate.  If neither finite record is present, promoting a
7640: bundle-local path to an unconditional submitted-support coordinate would add an
7641: evidence coordinate not contained in the support surface, contrary to
7642: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.  The stated
7643: conditional boundary follows for every bundle-backed surface named in the
7644: corollary.
7645: \end{proof}
7646: 
7647: \begin{corollary}[Fresh inventory digest instance for this source state]
7648: \label{cor:fresh-inventory-digest-instance-this-source-state}
7649: In the present source state, the regenerated files
7650: \path{theorem_inventory.json} and \path{theorem_inventory.md} are closed as local
7651: exact-inclusion coordinates only after their SHA-256 rows in
7652: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} are recomputed from the bytes of
7653: those two files and the finite command-boundary table names that refreshed
7654: manifest state.  This corollary supplies no upload receipt, archive locator,
7655: extractor-completeness certificate, or semantic proof check; it supplies only the
7656: local byte-coordinate instance required by
7657: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-theorem-inventory-digest-instance}.
7658: \end{corollary}
7659: 
7660: \begin{proof}
7661: There are two relevant file coordinates, namely
7662: \path{theorem_inventory.json} and \path{theorem_inventory.md}.  For each
7663: coordinate the SHA-256 computation is a deterministic function of the finite byte
7664: string stored at that relative path.  Replacing the rows in
7665: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} by the values computed after the
7666: inventory regeneration therefore makes the digest manifest name the regenerated
7667: bytes rather than a prior or anticipated inventory state.  The command-boundary
7668: table is the only place in the manuscript where command-run and freshness claims
7669: are promoted beyond path names, so updating that table to the same refreshed
7670: state prevents a stale 2026-06-05 package description from being read as the
7671: current digest instance.  The argument uses only byte equality at the two named
7672: paths and the finite-record boundary of
7673: Theorem~\ref{thm:digest-closure-refresh-criterion}; it does not add an extractor
7674: run, external support-surface instantiation, upload event, archive event, or
7675: semantic validation.  Hence exactly the local digest-closure claim stated above
7676: is obtained.
7677: \end{proof}
7678: 
7679: \begin{theorem}[Finite-record digest-refresh invariant for final package claims]
7680: \label{thm:digest-closure-refresh-criterion}
7681: For the current presentation package, any claim that a submitted support surface
7682: has exact final digests is accepted only for a closed finite state $D$ satisfying
7683: all of the following conditions.
7684: \begin{enumerate}
7685: \item The digest manifest \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} contains
7686: SHA-256 rows for \path{main.tex}, \path{submission_abstract.tex},
7687: \path{references.bib}, \path{source_interface_record.json},
7688: \path{theorem_inventory.json}, \path{theorem_inventory.md}, and every file in
7689: the selected \path{review_bundle/} support directory.
7690: \item The digest rows are computed after the accepted versions of
7691: \path{theorem_inventory.json}, \path{theorem_inventory.md},
7692: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, and every verifier log named
7693: by a command-run claim have been written.
7694: \item If \path{submission_abstract.tex} is edited after the primary-claim
7695: inventory was checked, then
7696: \path{review_bundle/verify_primary_claim_inventory.py} is rerun and its fresh
7697: log replaces the old primary-inventory freshness coordinate before the final
7698: digest rows are recomputed.
7699: \item If an upload-time venue-compliance claim, rather than a dated local
7700: readiness observation, is made, the venue and bibliography check is rerun at the
7701: upload rule state and the fresh venue record is included before the final digest
7702: rows are recomputed.
7703: \end{enumerate}
7704: If any one of these conditions fails, the package still has the local
7705: path-verified records printed in the manuscript, but it does not have an exact
7706: final-digest certificate for the regenerated inventory, primary-claim, or
7707: upload-time venue component that failed the condition.
7708: \end{theorem}
7709: 
7710: \begin{proof}
7711: The support-surface certificate is byte-level: by
7712: Theorem~\ref{thm:support-surface-normal-form}, exact inclusion and
7713: archive-equivalent inclusion both read the finite file list, relative paths, and
7714: SHA-256 content rows rather than prose descriptions of those files.  Therefore a
7715: file written after the digest manifest was computed cannot be covered by that
7716: manifest; its byte string was not one of the inputs to the listed hash values.
7717: This proves the necessity of recomputing the digest rows after regenerating the
7718: theorem inventory, primary-claim inventory, and command logs named by the final
7719: package.
7720: 
7721: The primary-claim inventory is line-span dependent.  Its verifier reads the
7722: current \path{submission_abstract.tex}, checks the row-id set and line ranges,
7723: and prints a finite freshness log.  If the primary source is changed after that
7724: run, at least the source line-span premise of the old log is no longer the
7725: current primary source checked by the verifier.  A fresh verifier run before the
7726: digest pass restores a finite coordinate for the new primary state; omitting it
7727: leaves only the old path-verified inventory observation.
7728: 
7729: Venue compliance is external-state dependent by
7730: Proposition~\ref{audit:fresh-venue-record-criterion} and
7731: Theorem~\ref{thm:venue-rule-freshness-non-escalation}.  A dated venue log can
7732: witness local readiness at its observation time, but upload-time compliance
7733: requires a record of the rule state at upload or an included replacement record.
7734: Thus a package that claims upload-time venue compliance must write that fresh
7735: record before the digest closure step; otherwise the digest manifest can cover
7736: only the dated local-readiness file, not the missing upload-time premise.
7737: 
7738: Combining these observations gives a finite fixed-point condition: first write
7739: the accepted inventory files and freshness logs, then compute the manifest over
7740: those bytes, and finally submit either the exact directory or an
7741: archive-equivalent replacement.  If a later edit or upload-time claim changes one
7742: of the inputs, the fixed point is broken precisely at that finite subrecord.  The
7743: remaining local rows remain available at their declared evidence levels, but the
7744: stronger exact final-digest claim is not accepted for the changed component.
7745: \end{proof}
7746: 
7747: 
7748: \begin{theorem}[Finite-record submission-support refresh invariant]
7749: \label{thm:submission-support-refresh-rigidity}
7750: Let $S$ be a source package for this presentation route, and suppose $S$ changes
7751: one of the following four support coordinates after the last accepted digest
7752: manifest was written:
7753: \begin{enumerate}
7754: \item the theorem-obligation inventory bytes in \path{theorem_inventory.json} or
7755: \path{theorem_inventory.md};
7756: \item a verifier or extractor log used to support a command-result claim;
7757: \item the primary-source line-span coordinate checked by
7758: \path{review_bundle/verify_primary_claim_inventory.py}; or
7759: \item the asserted venue-status coordinate, upgraded from dated local readiness to
7760: upload-time venue compliance.
7761: \end{enumerate}
7762: Then the strongest certificate available from the old manifest is obtained by
7763: deleting the changed coordinate from the claimed support tuple.  The changed
7764: coordinate re-enters the publication-safety chain only after the corresponding
7765: post-change extractor, verifier, or venue-rule record is written and the digest
7766: manifest is regenerated over those new bytes.  In particular, a post-inventory
7767: edit cannot be certified by a pre-inventory digest row, a dated local-readiness
7768: venue log cannot certify upload-time compliance, and a local bundle manifest
7769: cannot certify external upload or archive equivalence without the separate
7770: external coordinate of Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}.
7771: \end{theorem}
7772: 
7773: \begin{proof}
7774: All four listed coordinates are finite byte or rule-state fields in the support
7775: surface.  For item~1, the theorem-obligation ledger is exactly the ordered pair
7776: \path{theorem_inventory.json}, \path{theorem_inventory.md}, together with the
7777: extractor coverage row that reads those files.  If either inventory file is
7778: edited after the digest manifest was computed, the old SHA--256 row is a hash of
7779: a different byte string.  Since SHA--256 rows in this paper are used only as
7780: content-addressed finite records, not as semantic proof obligations, the old row
7781: cannot denote the new inventory bytes.  The coordinate is therefore absent until
7782: the extractor is rerun on the new inventory and the digest table is recomputed
7783: after that new log exists.
7784: 
7785: For item~2, Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} and
7786: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-command-result-closure} make every command-result claim
7787: depend on the finite tuple consisting of command, source state, environment,
7788: exit code, and log path.  A changed extractor or verifier log changes the log
7789: byte string selected by that tuple.  The old manifest can still witness the old
7790: run as historical local evidence, but it cannot be a digest certificate for the
7791: new run because the new log was not an input to the old hash table.
7792: 
7793: For item~3, the primary-claim verifier reads the current
7794: \path{submission_abstract.tex} and records row and line-span facts.  If the
7795: primary source changes after that verifier run, the line-span coordinate checked
7796: by the old log is no longer the current primary-source coordinate.  The only
7797: finite repair is to rerun the primary-claim verifier and then include the fresh
7798: log in the regenerated digest manifest.
7799: 
7800: For item~4, venue compliance is a time- and rule-state-dependent coordinate by
7801: Proposition~\ref{audit:fresh-venue-record-criterion} and
7802: Theorem~\ref{thm:venue-rule-freshness-non-escalation}.  A dated local-readiness
7803: record states what was observed at its logged date.  Upload-time compliance is a
7804: different finite field: it requires the rule state and submission state at the
7805: upload event.  Therefore an old local-readiness log cannot be reinterpreted as
7806: that upload-time field; a fresh venue-rule record must be written before digest
7807: closure if the stronger claim is made.
7808: 
7809: Finally, external upload or archive equivalence is independent of the local
7810: inventory and digest coordinates.  Theorems~\ref{thm:upload-or-archive-instantiation-boundary}
7811: and Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record} require either an
7812: exact supplemental-upload record or a byte-equality archive locator.  None of the
7813: four local refresh steps supplies such an external coordinate.  Thus the old
7814: manifest proves the package only after projecting away the changed or missing
7815: field, and the full support tuple is restored exactly by writing the required
7816: post-change finite records and regenerating the manifest over them.
7817: \end{proof}
7818: 
7819: 
7820: \begin{proposition}[Primary architectural fallback without external support]
7821: \label{prop:primary-architectural-fallback-without-external-support}
7822: Assume the current source package is used without an upload receipt for the exact
7823: \path{review_bundle/} directory and without an archive-equivalent replacement
7824: satisfying Audit record~\ref{audit:review-bundle-archive-equivalence}.  Then the
7825: primary presentation article may assert only the source-level architecture and
7826: path-verified local-package claims whose evidence surfaces are visible outside the
7827: missing bundle.  In particular, the theorem-ledger claim of
7828: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}, the case-snapshot claims,
7829: the verifier-log claims, and any claim whose certificate coordinate is
7830: \path{theorem_inventory.json} or a file under \path{review_bundle/} cannot be
7831: read as externally submitted evidence.  If either accepted external instantiation
7832: record is later supplied, the same claims re-enter exactly at the finite evidence
7833: level stated in their certificate rows and not at any stronger semantic or venue
7834: acceptance level.
7835: \end{proposition}
7836: 
7837: \begin{proof}
7838: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-no-receipt-support-surface-non-escalation} identifies
7839: the only two records that can externally instantiate a bundle-backed support
7840: claim: an exact upload receipt for \path{review_bundle/} with the final digest
7841: manifest, or an archive-equivalent locator passing the finite file-list, digest,
7842: manifest, and path-semantics tests.  Under the present hypothesis neither record
7843: is available.  Hence every bundle-local path is absent from the externally
7844: submitted finite support surface.
7845: 
7846: The affected claims are precisely those whose certificate coordinate names such a
7847: path.  Table~\ref{tab:submitted-theorem-obligation-ledger} places the manual
7848: theorem inventory at \path{theorem_inventory.json} and exposes it through
7849: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json}; the case-snapshot and
7850: verifier-log statements similarly name files under \path{review_bundle/}.  By
7851: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, a claim cannot acquire
7852: an evidence coordinate that is absent from the finite support surface.  Therefore
7853: those claims cannot be read as externally submitted evidence while both accepted
7854: instantiation records are missing.
7855: 
7856: Removing that external reading does not remove the primary article's independent
7857: architectural assertion.  The primary text describes the compiler's typed record
7858: interface and cites the pinned public source surface; those claims do not require
7859: the missing bundle-local coordinate.  If a valid exact-upload or
7860: archive-equivalent record is later supplied, Theorem~\ref{thm:support-surface-normal-form}
7861: and Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record} put the
7862: bundle-backed claims back into the finite support surface.  The reinstatement is
7863: only at the recorded evidence levels, because the same corollaries and
7864: Theorem~\ref{thm:upload-or-archive-instantiation-boundary} exclude stronger
7865: readings such as semantic proof checking, fresh dynamic validation, venue
7866: compliance, or acceptance.\end{proof}
