7886: \begin{theorem}[Deterministic theorem-environment extractor boundary]
7887: \label{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary}
7888: Let the theorem-like environment domain $\mathcal E$ for this package consist
7889: of the five environment names \path{definition}, \path{lemma},
7890: \path{proposition}, \path{theorem}, and \path{corollary}.  The command
7891: \path{python review_bundle/extract_theorem_environments.py} run on
7892: \path{main.tex} and \path{theorem_inventory.json} is a deterministic
7893: syntactic extractor for the following finite claim only: every labelled
7894: environment in \path{main.tex} whose environment name belongs to $\mathcal E$ is
7895: listed by label in \path{theorem_inventory.json}; every such environment is
7896: labelled; and no such label is duplicated.  When the refreshed run log
7897: \path{review_bundle/theorem_environment_extraction_run.log} records exit code
7898: \path{0}, the package has a deterministic theorem-environment coverage
7899: certificate at this syntactic label level.  The certificate does not assert that
7900: the inventory is a semantic proof checker, that the printed proofs are correct,